The table structure information is (table names): 
['problem', 'eda', 'feature_importance', 'solution', 'model_score', 'model_importance', 'model']
Begin Exploring Related Columns
Relevant columns and their potential usage  
• model.name: join key to identify a dataset/model family  
• model.version: join key (together with name) to distinguish a concrete “model” instance  
• model.step: makes each level‑1 model (base learner) unique inside a given (name, version) run  
• model.L1_model: the level‑1 algorithm we must finally count → “What appears most often for strong / soft”  
• model_score.name / model_score.version / model_score.step: foreign keys to line‑up with model table  
• model_score.model: tells us which concrete learner was evaluated in every step; needed to recognise rows whose model LIKE '%Stack%' (= level‑2 stacker) versus other learners (= non‑Stack)  
• model_score.test_score: score we must compare between Stack learner and the best non‑Stack learner inside the same (name, version, step) record set  

Typical conditions we will need  
• model_score.model LIKE '%Stack%' -- pick the stacker rows  
• model_score.model NOT LIKE '%Stack%' -- pick non‑stacker rows  
• HAVING MAX(CASE WHEN …) < MAX(CASE WHEN …) -- decide “strong” (or = for “soft”)  
• GROUP BY name, version -- one status for every (name, version) run  

Exploratory SQLs
```sql
-- 1. List all distinct datasets (name) present in the modelling tables
SELECT DISTINCT "name"
FROM "model"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. List all distinct (name, version) pairs that have both model and score information
SELECT DISTINCT m."name",
                m."version"
FROM   "model"        AS m
JOIN   "model_score"  AS s USING ("name","version","step")
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Inspect all distinct values that appear in model_score.model
SELECT DISTINCT "model"
FROM   "model_score"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Show a sample of scores where a 'Stack' model is present
SELECT  ms."name",
        ms."version",
        ms."step",
        ms."model",
        ms."test_score"
FROM    "model_score" AS ms
WHERE   ms."model" LIKE '%Stack%'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Compare test scores of Stack vs non‑Stack models inside the same (name, version, step)
SELECT  ms1."name",
        ms1."version",
        ms1."step",
        MAX(CASE WHEN ms1."model" LIKE '%Stack%'     THEN ms1."test_score" END) AS stack_score,
        MAX(CASE WHEN ms1."model" NOT LIKE '%Stack%' THEN ms1."test_score" END) AS best_non_stack
FROM    "model_score" AS ms1
GROUP BY ms1."name", ms1."version", ms1."step"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Flag every (name, version, step) as 'strong', 'soft' or 'none' (no Stack found)
SELECT  t.*,
        CASE
             WHEN stack_score IS NULL                         THEN 'none'
             WHEN best_non_stack <  stack_score               THEN 'strong'
             WHEN best_non_stack =  stack_score               THEN 'soft'
        END AS status_step
FROM (
      SELECT  ms."name",
              ms."version",
              ms."step",
              MAX(CASE WHEN ms."model" LIKE '%Stack%'     THEN ms."test_score" END) AS stack_score,
              MAX(CASE WHEN ms."model" NOT LIKE '%Stack%' THEN ms."test_score" END) AS best_non_stack
      FROM    "model_score" AS ms
      GROUP BY ms."name", ms."version", ms."step"
) AS t
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Aggregate step‑level flags into one run‑level status ('strong' if ANY strong step, else 'soft' if ANY soft step)
WITH step_flag AS (
      SELECT  ms."name",
              ms."version",
              MAX(CASE WHEN ms."model" LIKE '%Stack%'     THEN ms."test_score" END) AS stack_score,
              MAX(CASE WHEN ms."model" NOT LIKE '%Stack%' THEN ms."test_score" END) AS best_non_stack
      FROM    "model_score" AS ms
      GROUP BY ms."name", ms."version", ms."step"
), step_status AS (
      SELECT  "name",
              "version",
              CASE
                   WHEN best_non_stack < stack_score THEN 'strong'
                   WHEN best_non_stack = stack_score THEN 'soft'
              END AS status_step
      FROM    step_flag
)
SELECT  "name",
        "version",
        CASE
             WHEN SUM(status_step='strong') > 0 THEN 'strong'
             WHEN SUM(status_step='soft')   > 0 THEN 'soft'
        END AS run_status
FROM    step_status
GROUP BY "name","version"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Attach L1_model to every (name, version) run_status
WITH run_status AS (
      /* reuse CTE from query 7 */
      WITH step_flag AS (
            SELECT  ms."name",
                    ms."version",
                    MAX(CASE WHEN ms."model" LIKE '%Stack%'     THEN ms."test_score" END) AS stack_score,
                    MAX(CASE WHEN ms."model" NOT LIKE '%Stack%' THEN ms."test_score" END) AS best_non_stack
            FROM    "model_score" AS ms
            GROUP BY ms."name", ms."version", ms."step"
      ), step_status AS (
            SELECT  "name","version",
                    CASE
                         WHEN best_non_stack < stack_score THEN 'strong'
                         WHEN best_non_stack = stack_score THEN 'soft'
                    END AS status_step
            FROM    step_flag
      )
      SELECT  "name","version",
              CASE
                   WHEN SUM(status_step='strong') > 0 THEN 'strong'
                   WHEN SUM(status_step='soft')   > 0 THEN 'soft'
              END AS run_status
      FROM    step_status
      GROUP BY "name","version"
)
SELECT  rs."name",
        rs."version",
        rs."run_status",
        m."L1_model"
FROM    run_status AS rs
JOIN    "model"      AS m
       ON m."name" = rs."name"
      AND m."version" = rs."version"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
